t going to go into a  massive preamble about the start of the  civil war that has more to do with  caesar than it does to do with pompeii  and we already have a bio on julius  caesar if you want that story in full  suffice to say that in 49 bc caesar  crossed the rubicon after winning the  gaelic wars he refused a demand from the  senate to disband his troops and marched  into italy with an army from that point  on civil war became inevitable all those  years the senate feared that this was  something that pompey would do although  he never did and now that caesar had  done it they rushed to pompei for help  he agreed but was caught off guard by  the rapid pace at which caesar advanced  toward rome when the two sides first met  in battle at the siege of brundicium an  outmanned and ill-prepared pompei  suffered one of his greatest defeats  this gave pompei the dark realization  that he could not defend rome so he  abandoned the city and fled to macedonia  to properly mobilize his forces he set  up his camp in the city of diraccia in  modern-day albania caesar besieged the  city in 48 bc but after months of  inconclusive skirmishes he conceded  defeat and made a strategic retreat thus  tying their score at one all the great  decider occurred in august of that year  at the battle of fasalas in central  greece pompei didn't want to do it he  thought he was rushing things but his  allies all pressured him to meet up with  a confederate named metallia scepio and  take out caesar but that's not how  things went down pompeii's plan was to  make use of his large cavalry attack  caesar's right wing from the flank go  around the rear of the enemy and trap  them between two armies however caesar  shrewdly guessed pompey's strategy and  reinforced his right wing but kept his  extra soldiers out of sight until it was  too late for pompeii's men they pounced  at the right time and brought chaos  confusion and death to pompei's cavalry  units they retreated in a panic and from  th